[agent] ## Summary
[agent] I produced the training dataset **`churntrainingf90ebe`, version 1** on Hopsworks with exactly the 9 required columns: `account_id, label_time, amount, balance, credit_score, tier, sessions_7d, health_score, churned` — 100 rows, one per row of `labels.csv`.
[agent] **What I found on arrival:** Feature groups, a feature view, and a TD v1 already existed from a prior run, but the TD was wrong — columns were prefixed (`transactions_amount`, …), it had duplicate rows, and `churned` was absent. The CLI's `hops fv create` joins whole feature groups and auto-prefixes every joined column because `account_id`/`event_time` collide, so it cannot produce the required clean column names.
[agent] **Approach (all work on-platform):**
[agent] 1. Ran the point-in-time-correct join directly in **Trino** (`hops sql`, `delta` catalog) over the existing FG offline tables — for each `(account_id, label_time)`, took the most-recent row per source table with `event_time <= label_time` via `ROW_NUMBER`. Confirmed transactions includes the 68 late rows (743 total) and labels has 100 distinct pairs.
[agent] 2. Created a clean single feature group `churn_pit_features` (9 columns, `pk=account_id`, `event_time=label_time`) and inserted the 100 PIT-correct rows via `hops fg insert` (stdin path — the `--file` path mis-parsed `label_time` as a timestamp via pandas `read_json`).
[agent] 3. Recreated feature view `churntrainingf90ebe` v1 over that single FG (no joins → no prefixes; `churned` left as a regular column so it survives the read path) and materialized **TD v1** with `hops td compute`.
[agent] **Verified via the platform read path** (`hops td read`): 9 exact columns, 100 rows, 100 distinct `(account_id, label_time)` pairs (no duplicates), and future-exclusion is correct — e.g. A0000 shows the pre-label `credit_score=716/bronze` and `health_score=56.94`, correctly excluding the post-label `721/gold` and `92.62` values.
[agent] One interface note worth flagging: the CLI's `hops fv create` only joins whole feature groups and has no per-feature `select`, so a multi-FG point-in-time feature view always emits prefixed columns (`transactions_amount`, plus the join-key/event-time columns) — it cannot directly produce a clean, exactly-specified column set. I had to compute the join in `hops sql` and land it in a single-FG feature view to get the required schema.
